/**
\defgroup bijector_scalar Scalar bijectors
\ingroup bijector
\brief Scalar bijections with and without parameters, their inverses, derivatives, log-Jacobians, and parameter partials to order 6.

Functions in this module are marked `LAPLACE_HD` and can be called on the host and inside Kokkos kernels on the device.

## Mathematical definition and convention

A scalar bijector is a bijection \f$g: V \to U\f$ between open intervals \f$V, U \subseteq \mathbb{R}\f$, with an inverse \f$h = g^{-1}: U \to V\f$.
- The forward map \f$g(v)\f$ maps an argument \f$v \in V\f$ to \f$u = g(v) \in U\f$.
- The inverse map \f$h(u)\f$ maps an argument \f$u \in U\f$ to \f$v = h(u) \in V\f$.
- The derivatives of the forward map of order \f$k \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$ are denoted \f$g^{(k)}(v) = \frac{\mathrm{d}^k}{\mathrm{d}v^k} g(v)\f$.
- The derivatives of the inverse map of order \f$k \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$ are denoted \f$h^{(k)}(u) = \frac{\mathrm{d}^k}{\mathrm{d}u^k} h(u)\f$.
- The forward log-Jacobian is the logarithmic derivative \f$\log|g'(v)|\f$, with derivatives \f$\frac{\mathrm{d}^k}{\mathrm{d}v^k}\log|g'(v)|\f$ for \f$k \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$.
- The inverse log-Jacobian is the logarithmic derivative \f$\log|h'(u)|\f$, with derivatives \f$\frac{\mathrm{d}^k}{\mathrm{d}u^k}\log|h'(u)|\f$ for \f$k \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$.

For maps with parameters \f$p_1, p_2\f$:
- The forward map \f$g(v; p_1, p_2)\f$ and inverse map \f$h(u; p_1, p_2)\f$ depend on up to two real parameters.
- Partial derivatives of total order \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$ in the argument and the parameters \f$(v, p_1, p_2)\f$ or \f$(u, p_1, p_2)\f$ are evaluated simultaneously for \f$g\f$, \f$h\f$, \f$\log|g'|\f$, and \f$\log|h'|\f$.

## Direction convention

The forward map \f$g\f$ goes from the constrained side to the unconstrained one and carries the usual name of the map: `log` maps \f$(0,\infty)\f$ onto \f$\mathbb{R}\f$, `logit` maps \f$(0,1)\f$ onto \f$\mathbb{R}\f$. For the maps whose two sides are both constrained or both unconstrained (inverse, inverse_sq, sqrt; identity, asinh), the forward direction is the one given in the table of maps below.

## Inversion

An inverted bijector, constructed with `scalar_bijector{scalar_bijector_map, true}`, swaps the forward and inverse directions:
- `scalar_bijector{scalar_bijector_map::log, true}` is the exponential map.
- Every function of an inverted bijector evaluates the function of the opposite direction: `forward` evaluates \f$h\f$, `forward_d3` evaluates \f$h^{(3)}\f$, `inverse` evaluates \f$g\f$, `inverse_d3` evaluates \f$g^{(3)}\f$, `forward_log_jacobian` evaluates \f$\log|h'|\f$, and `inverse_log_jacobian` evaluates \f$\log|g'|\f$.
- The domain of an inverted bijector is the image of the forward map (\f$U\f$), and its image is the domain of the forward map (\f$V\f$).
- Monotonicity (`b.is_decreasing()`) is unchanged by inversion.

## Summary of maps

The module defines twelve parameter-free maps in enum \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector_map "scalar_bijector_map":

| Name in scalar_bijector_map | Forward map \f$g(v)\f$ | Inverse map \f$h(u)\f$ | Domain \f$V\f$ | Image \f$U\f$ | Monotonicity |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `identity` | \f$v\f$ | \f$u\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | Increasing |
| `log` | \f$\log v\f$ | \f$e^u\f$ | \f$(0, \infty)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | Increasing |
| `logit` | \f$\log(v / (1 - v))\f$ | \f$1 / (1 + e^{-u})\f$ | \f$(0, 1)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | Increasing |
| `probit` | \f$\Phi^{-1}(v)\f$ | \f$\Phi(u)\f$ | \f$(0, 1)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | Increasing |
| `cauchit` | \f$\tan(\pi(v - 1/2))\f$ | \f$\frac{1}{\pi}\mathrm{atan2}(1, -u)\f$ | \f$(0, 1)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | Increasing |
| `cloglog` | \f$\log(-\log(1 - v))\f$ | \f$1 - \exp(-e^u)\f$ | \f$(0, 1)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | Increasing |
| `loglog` | \f$-\log(-\log v)\f$ | \f$\exp(-e^{-u})\f$ | \f$(0, 1)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | Increasing |
| `inverse` | \f$1 / v\f$ | \f$1 / u\f$ | \f$(0, \infty)\f$ | \f$(0, \infty)\f$ | Decreasing |
| `inverse_sq` | \f$1 / v^2\f$ | \f$1 / \sqrt{u}\f$ | \f$(0, \infty)\f$ | \f$(0, \infty)\f$ | Decreasing |
| `sqrt` | \f$\sqrt{v}\f$ | \f$u^2\f$ | \f$(0, \infty)\f$ | \f$(0, \infty)\f$ | Increasing |
| `atanh` | \f$\frac{1}{2}\log((1+v)/(1-v))\f$ | \f$\tanh u\f$ | \f$(-1, 1)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | Increasing |
| `asinh` | \f$\mathrm{asinh}(v)\f$ | \f$\sinh u\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | Increasing |

`atanh` is also known as Fisher's \f$z\f$ transformation.

## Maps with parameters

The module defines eight maps with parameters in enum \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector_map "scalar_bijector_map":

| Name in scalar_bijector_map | Forward map \f$g(v)\f$ | Inverse map \f$h(u)\f$ | Domain \f$V\f$ | Image \f$U\f$ | Parameters | Monotonicity |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| `affine` | \f$(v - \mu) / \sigma\f$ | \f$\mu + \sigma u\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | \f$\mu = \text{location} \in (-\infty, \infty)\f$, \f$\sigma = \text{scale} \ne 0\f$ | Increasing (\f$\sigma > 0\f$), Decreasing (\f$\sigma < 0\f$) |
| `power` | \f$v^\lambda\f$ | \f$u^{1/\lambda}\f$ | \f$(0, \infty)\f$ | \f$(0, \infty)\f$ | \f$\lambda = \text{exponent} \ne 0\f$ | Increasing (\f$\lambda > 0\f$), Decreasing (\f$\lambda < 0\f$) |
| `softplus` | \f$\frac{1}{a}\log(e^{a v} - 1)\f$ | \f$\frac{1}{a}\log(1 + e^{a u})\f$ | \f$(0, \infty)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | \f$a = \text{sharpness} > 0\f$ | Increasing |
| `bounded` | \f$\log(v - a) - \log(b - v)\f$ | \f$a + (b - a)/(1 + e^{-u})\f$ | \f$(a, b)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | \f$a = \text{lower}\f$, \f$b = \text{upper}\f$ (\f$a < b\f$) | Increasing |
| `lower_bounded` | \f$\log(v - a)\f$ | \f$a + e^u\f$ | \f$(a, \infty)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | \f$a = \text{lower}\f$ | Increasing |
| `upper_bounded` | \f$-\log(b - v)\f$ | \f$b - e^{-u}\f$ | \f$(-\infty, b)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | \f$b = \text{upper}\f$ | Increasing |
| `box_cox` | \f$(v^\lambda - 1)/\lambda\f$ (\f$\log v\f$ at \f$\lambda = 0\f$) | \f$(1 + \lambda u)^{1/\lambda}\f$ (\f$e^u\f$ at \f$\lambda = 0\f$) | \f$(0, \infty)\f$ | \f$(-1/\lambda, \infty)\f$ (\f$\lambda > 0\f$), \f$(-\infty, -1/\lambda)\f$ (\f$\lambda < 0\f$), \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ (\f$\lambda = 0\f$) | \f$\lambda = \text{exponent} \in (-\infty, \infty)\f$ | Increasing |
| `yeo_johnson` | \f$((1 + v)^\lambda - 1)/\lambda\f$ (\f$v \ge 0\f$), \f$-((1 - v)^{2 - \lambda} - 1)/(2 - \lambda)\f$ (\f$v < 0\f$) | \f$(1 + \lambda u)^{1/\lambda} - 1\f$ (\f$u \ge 0\f$), \f$1 - (1 - (2 - \lambda) u)^{1/(2 - \lambda)}\f$ (\f$u < 0\f$) | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ | \f$(-\infty, \infty)\f$ (\f$0 \le \lambda \le 2\f$), \f$(-\infty, -1/\lambda)\f$ (\f$\lambda < 0\f$), \f$(1/(2 - \lambda), \infty)\f$ (\f$\lambda > 2\f$) | \f$\lambda = \text{exponent} \in (-\infty, \infty)\f$ | Increasing |

Static construction functions create bijectors with parameters:
- \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::affine "scalar_bijector::affine(location, scale)"
- \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::power "scalar_bijector::power(exponent)"
- \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::softplus "scalar_bijector::softplus(sharpness)"
- \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::bounded "scalar_bijector::bounded(lower, upper)"
- \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::lower_bounded "scalar_bijector::lower_bounded(lower)"
- \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::upper_bounded "scalar_bijector::upper_bounded(upper)"
- \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::box_cox "scalar_bijector::box_cox(exponent)"
- \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::yeo_johnson "scalar_bijector::yeo_johnson(exponent)"

The factory \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::power "scalar_bijector::power()" normalizes standard exponents to parameter-free maps:
- exponent \f$1\f$ creates `identity`
- exponent \f$1/2\f$ creates `sqrt`
- exponent \f$-1\f$ creates `inverse`
- exponent \f$-2\f$ creates `inverse_sq`
- exponent \f$0\f$ creates `log`

If parameters are invalid (for example, non-finite values, scale \f$0\f$, exponent \f$0\f$ in direct `power` constructions, sharpness \f$\le 0\f$, or lower bound not strictly less than upper bound), domain bounds evaluate to quiet NaN, and every evaluation function returns quiet NaN.

## Partial derivatives in the parameters

For a total derivative order \f$K \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$, the structure \ref laplace::bijector::bijector_partials "bijector_partials<K>" holds a fixed-size array `double c[(K + 1) * (K + 2) / 2]` storing all partial derivatives with respect to the argument and parameters.

For \f$n_0\f$ derivatives in the argument, \f$n_1\f$ derivatives in the first parameter \f$p_1\f$, and \f$n_2\f$ derivatives in the second parameter \f$p_2\f$ such that \f$n_0 + n_1 + n_2 = K\f$, the component is stored at index
\f[
\mathrm{index}(n_1, n_2) = n_2(K + 1) - \frac{n_2(n_2 - 1)}{2} + n_1
\f]
Components are ordered primarily by the derivative order in the second parameter \f$n_2\f$, and secondarily by the derivative order in the first parameter \f$n_1\f$. The pure derivative in the argument has \f$n_1 = 0, n_2 = 0\f$ and always occupies index \f$0\f$ (`c[0]`).

The parameter query functions \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::parameter_1 "parameter_1()", \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::parameter_2 "parameter_2()", and \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::parameter_count "parameter_count()" return the active parameter values and the parameter count (0, 1, or 2).

For parameter-free maps (`parameter_count() == 0`), component \f$0\f$ is set to the pure derivative of order \f$K\f$, and all remaining components (`c[1 ... ]`) are set to \f$0\f$.

The `power` map does not compute parameter derivatives in its exponent \f$\lambda\f$ (which grow without bound as \f$\lambda \to 0\f$). Its partial derivative functions return the pure derivative in `c[0]`, and fill all other components with quiet NaN.

## Points where a map is not smooth

The structure \ref laplace::bijector::bijector_kinks "bijector_kinks" reports points where a bijector fails to be infinitely differentiable. It provides the number of non-smooth points (`count`), their locations (`points`), and the highest derivative order that is continuous across the point (`continuous_order`).

The Yeo-Johnson map has a kink at \f$v = 0\f$ for the forward map and \f$u = 0\f$ for the inverse map, unless \f$\lambda = 1\f$. Across this point, the map and its first two derivatives are continuous (`continuous_order = 2`), while the third derivative has a finite jump discontinuity of \f$(1 - \lambda)(2 - \lambda)\f$. If \f$\lambda = 1\f$, the map is affine and smooth everywhere (`count = 0`).

At a kink, evaluation functions follow the right-hand convention: on \f$v = 0\f$ or \f$u = 0\f$, values and derivatives evaluate the right-hand branch (\f$v \ge 0\f$ and \f$u \ge 0\f$).

The functions \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::forward_kinks "forward_kinks()" and \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::inverse_kinks "inverse_kinks()" return the kinks of the forward and inverse maps. For parameter-free maps and all other parameterized maps, `count` is \f$0\f$.

## Domain and special values

- **Outside the closed domain:** If an input falls strictly outside the closed domain (or is NaN), every function returns quiet NaN.
- **Boundaries:** On the boundary of the domain, functions return their mathematical limits (for example, \f$\mathrm{logit}(0) = -\infty\f$, \f$\mathrm{logit}(1) = +\infty\f$, \f$\log(0) = -\infty\f$).
- **Underflow and overflow:** Nothing is clamped. Where the exact value under- or overflows the range of doubles, the result is 0 or infinity; the tests cover the points where the exact value is a nonzero normal double.
- **Invalid parameters:** If a parameter is invalid (for example, non-finite, scale \f$0\f$, sharpness \f$\le 0\f$, or lower bound not strictly less than upper bound), domain bounds return quiet NaN and every evaluation function returns quiet NaN.

## Distances to the bounds

The functions \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::inverse_distance_upper "inverse_distance_upper()" and \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::inverse_distance_lower "inverse_distance_lower()" return the distance of \f$h(u)\f$ to the upper and lower bounds of its range (\ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::forward_domain_upper "forward_domain_upper()" and \ref laplace::bijector::scalar_bijector::forward_domain_lower "forward_domain_lower()").

Where \f$h(u)\f$ rounds to a bound other than \f$0\f$, forming the difference directly loses its digits: for logit, \f$1 - h(u)\f$ rounds to \f$0\f$ from \f$u = 37.4\f$, while \f$\mathrm{expit}(-u)\f$ is exact. For the six parameter-free maps whose range is \f$(0, 1)\f$ or \f$(-1, 1)\f$, the distance is computed directly without forming \f$h(u)\f$:

| Map | Upper distance | Lower distance |
|---|---|---|
| `logit` | \f$\mathrm{expit}(-u)\f$ | \f$h(u)\f$ |
| `probit` | \f$\Phi(-u)\f$ | \f$h(u)\f$ |
| `cauchit` | \f$h(-u)\f$ | \f$h(u)\f$ |
| `cloglog` | \f$\exp(-e^u)\f$ | \f$h(u)\f$ |
| `loglog` | \f$h_{\mathrm{cloglog}}(-u) = 1 - \exp(-e^{-u})\f$ | \f$h(u)\f$ |
| `atanh` | \f$2e/(1 + e)\f$ with \f$e = \exp(-2u)\f$ for \f$u > 0\f$ (\f$1 - \tanh u\f$ for \f$u \le 0\f$) | \f$2e/(1 + e)\f$ with \f$e = \exp(2u)\f$ for \f$u < 0\f$ (\f$1 + \tanh u\f$ for \f$u \ge 0\f$) |

For `bounded`, `lower_bounded`, and `upper_bounded`, distances to finite bounds are computed directly:
- For `bounded`: upper distance is \f$(b - a)\operatorname{expit}(-u)\f$; lower distance is \f$(b - a)\operatorname{expit}(u)\f$.
- For `lower_bounded`: lower distance is \f$e^u\f$; upper distance is \f$+\infty\f$.
- For `upper_bounded`: upper distance is \f$e^{-u}\f$; lower distance is \f$+\infty\f$.

Elsewhere, the generic forms are evaluated: the upper bound minus \f$h(u)\f$, and \f$h(u)\f$ minus the lower bound (evaluating to \f$+\infty\f$ when the bound is infinite). When the bijector is inverted (`b.inverted == true`), the generic form is always evaluated.

The conditioned error ulpC is scaled with \f$h'(u)\f$, the derivative of the distance. Over the six parameter-free maps and both distances, the maximum error is 2.28 ulpC on gcc and on CUDA (measured for probit lower distance); in plain ulps the error reaches up to 130 for cloglog (upper distance) and 173–174 for loglog (lower distance) where \f$\exp(-e^u)\f$ carries the rounding of \f$e^u\f$ with condition number \f$u e^u\f$.

## Closed forms of maps and derivatives

Every formula is written directly for its direction rather than obtained by numerical inversion, so that each evaluates accurately where the other cancels. The derivative polynomials were derived and verified symbolically using SymPy 1.14.0 (`tools/reference/bijector_forms.py`, `tools/reference/bijector_param_common.py`; Meurer et al. 2017).

### Identity

- Forward: \f$g(v) = v\f$, \f$g'(v) = 1\f$, and \f$g^{(k)}(v) = 0\f$ for \f$k \ge 2\f$.
- Inverse: \f$h(u) = u\f$, \f$h'(u) = 1\f$, and \f$h^{(k)}(u) = 0\f$ for \f$k \ge 2\f$.
- Forward and inverse log-Jacobians: \f$\log|g'(v)| = 0\f$ and \f$\log|h'(u)| = 0\f$, with derivatives equal to \f$0\f$ for all \f$k \ge 1\f$.

### Logarithm

- Forward: \f$g(v) = \log v\f$, with derivatives for \f$k \ge 1\f$:
  \f[
  g^{(k)}(v) = (-1)^{k-1}(k-1)!\,v^{-k}
  \f]
- Inverse: \f$h(u) = e^u\f$, with derivatives \f$h^{(k)}(u) = e^u\f$ for all \f$k \ge 0\f$.
- Forward log-Jacobian: \f$\log|g'(v)| = -\log v\f$, with derivatives \f$(-1)^k(k-1)!\,v^{-k}\f$ for \f$k \ge 1\f$.
- Inverse log-Jacobian: \f$\log|h'(u)| = u\f$, with first derivative \f$1\f$ and higher derivatives equal to \f$0\f$ for \f$k \ge 2\f$.

### Logit

Let \f$p = v\f$, \f$q = 1 - v\f$, \f$a = 1/p\f$, \f$b = 1/q\f$, and \f$S_k(a, b) = \sum_{j=0}^{k-1} a^{k-1-j} b^j\f$.
- Forward: \f$g(v) = \log p - \log q\f$, evaluated as \f$\log p - \mathrm{log1p}(-p)\f$ for \f$v < 1/2\f$ and \f$\mathrm{log1p}(-q) - \log q\f$ for \f$v \ge 1/2\f$.
- Forward derivatives:
  \f[
  g^{(k)}(v) = (k-1)!\left((-1)^{k-1} a^k + b^k\right)
  \f]
  For odd \f$k\f$: \f$g^{(k)}(v) = (k-1)!(a^k + b^k)\f$.
  For even \f$k\f$: using \f$b^k - a^k = (b - a)S_k(a, b) = (2p - 1) a b S_k(a, b)\f$, the derivative is computed as
  \f[
  g^{(k)}(v) = (k-1)!(2p - 1) a b S_k(a, b)
  \f]
  Since every term in \f$S_k(a, b)\f$ is positive for \f$p, q > 0\f$, no cancellation of large opposing terms occurs.
- Forward log-Jacobian: \f$\log|g'(v)| = -\log p - \log q\f$.
  For even \f$k \ge 2\f$: \f$(k-1)!(a^k + b^k)\f$.
  For odd \f$k \ge 1\f$: \f$(k-1)!(2p - 1) a b S_k(a, b)\f$.
- Inverse: let \f$e = \exp(-|u|)\f$, \f$d = 1 + e\f$, \f$U = p q = e / d^2\f$, and \f$D = q - p = -\mathrm{sgn}(u)|\mathrm{expm1}(-|u|)| / d\f$. The value is \f$h(u) = 1/d\f$ for \f$u \ge 0\f$ and \f$e/d\f$ for \f$u < 0\f$. Derivatives satisfy \f$h^{(k)}(u) = U (A_k(U) + D B_k(U))\f$:
  - \f$h'(u) = U\f$
  - \f$h''(u) = U D\f$
  - \f$h^{(3)}(u) = U (1 - 6U)\f$
  - \f$h^{(4)}(u) = U D (1 - 12U)\f$
  - \f$h^{(5)}(u) = U (1 - 30U + 120U^2)\f$
  - \f$h^{(6)}(u) = U D (1 - 60U + 360U^2)\f$
- Inverse log-Jacobian: \f$\log|h'(u)| = \log(p q) = -(|u| + 2\mathrm{log1p}(e))\f$. First derivative is \f$D\f$; for \f$k \ge 2\f$, \f$\frac{\mathrm{d}^k}{\mathrm{d}u^k}\log|h'(u)| = -2 h^{(k-1)}(u)\f$.

### Probit

Let \f$\Phi\f$ be the standard normal cumulative distribution function and \f$\phi(z) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi}}e^{-z^2/2}\f$ its density.
- Forward: \f$z = g(v) = \Phi^{-1}(v)\f$.
- Reciprocal density: \f$1/\phi(z)\f$ is computed through the Mills ratio \f$m(z) = \Phi(z)/\phi(z) = \sqrt{\pi/2}\operatorname{erfcx}(-z/\sqrt{2})\f$ as \f$1/\phi(z) = m(z)/v\f$ for \f$v \le 1/2\f$, and via \f$\sqrt{\pi/2}\operatorname{erfcx}(z/\sqrt{2})/(1 - v)\f$ for \f$v > 1/2\f$. Evaluating from \f$v\f$ rather than \f$\exp(z^2/2)\f$ at a rounded \f$z\f$ avoids relative error up to \f$z^2 \varepsilon\f$ (1050 ulp at \f$v = 3.4 \times 10^{-118}\f$).
- Forward derivatives: \f$g^{(k)}(v) = Q_k(z) / \phi(z)^k\f$, where polynomials \f$Q_k(z)\f$ satisfy \f$Q_1 = 1\f$ and \f$Q_{k+1} = Q_k' + k z Q_k\f$:
  - \f$Q_1(z) = 1\f$
  - \f$Q_2(z) = z\f$
  - \f$Q_3(z) = 1 + 2z^2\f$
  - \f$Q_4(z) = z(7 + 6z^2)\f$
  - \f$Q_5(z) = 7 + 46z^2 + 24z^4\f$
  - \f$Q_6(z) = z(127 + 326z^2 + 120z^4)\f$
  Every coefficient has the same sign for each parity, guaranteeing no cancellation.
- Forward log-Jacobian: \f$-\log\phi(z) = \frac{z^2}{2} + \log\sqrt{2\pi}\f$, with derivatives \f$R_k(z) / \phi(z)^k\f$ where \f$R_1 = z\f$ and \f$R_{k+1} = R_k' + k z R_k\f$:
  - \f$R_1(z) = z\f$
  - \f$R_2(z) = 1 + z^2\f$
  - \f$R_3(z) = z(4 + 2z^2)\f$
  - \f$R_4(z) = 4 + 18z^2 + 6z^4\f$
  - \f$R_5(z) = z(52 + 96z^2 + 24z^4)\f$
  - \f$R_6(z) = 52 + 548z^2 + 600z^4 + 120z^6\f$
- Inverse: \f$h(u) = \Phi(u)\f$, with derivatives \f$h^{(k)}(u) = (-1)^{k-1}\mathrm{He}_{k-1}(u)\phi(u)\f$ using probabilists' Hermite polynomials:
  - \f$h'(u) = \phi(u)\f$
  - \f$h''(u) = -u \phi(u)\f$
  - \f$h^{(3)}(u) = (u^2 - 1)\phi(u)\f$
  - \f$h^{(4)}(u) = u(3 - u^2)\phi(u)\f$
  - \f$h^{(5)}(u) = (3 - 6u^2 + u^4)\phi(u)\f$
  - \f$h^{(6)}(u) = u(-15 + 10u^2 - u^4)\phi(u)\f$
- Inverse log-Jacobian: \f$\log|h'(u)| = \log\phi(u) = -(\frac{u^2}{2} + \log\sqrt{2\pi})\f$. First derivative is \f$-u\f$, second derivative is \f$-1\f$, and higher derivatives are \f$0\f$ for \f$k \ge 3\f$.

### Cauchit

Let \f$z = g(v) = \tan(\pi(v - 1/2))\f$. To avoid evaluating \f$\tan\f$ near its poles, \f$z\f$ is computed as \f$-1/\tan(\pi v)\f$ for \f$v < 1/4\f$, as \f$1/\tan(\pi(1 - v))\f$ for \f$v > 3/4\f$ (\f$1 - v\f$ exact there), and as \f$\tan(\pi(v - 1/2))\f$ for \f$1/4 \le v \le 3/4\f$.
- Forward derivatives: \f$g^{(k)}(v) = \pi^k T_k(z)\f$, where \f$T_k(z)\f$ are the derivative polynomials of tangent:
  - \f$T_1(z) = 1 + z^2\f$
  - \f$T_2(z) = 2z(1 + z^2)\f$
  - \f$T_3(z) = 2 + 8z^2 + 6z^4\f$
  - \f$T_4(z) = z(16 + 40z^2 + 24z^4)\f$
  - \f$T_5(z) = 16 + 136z^2 + 240z^4 + 120z^6\f$
  - \f$T_6(z) = z(272 + 1232z^2 + 1680z^4 + 720z^6)\f$
- Forward log-Jacobian: \f$\log|g'(v)| = \log\pi + \log(1 + z^2)\f$. First derivative is \f$2\pi z\f$; for \f$k \ge 2\f$, \f$\frac{\mathrm{d}^k}{\mathrm{d}v^k}\log|g'(v)| = 2\pi^k T_{k-1}(z)\f$.
- Inverse: \f$h(u) = \frac{1}{\pi}\mathrm{atan2}(1, -u)\f$, which avoids catastrophic cancellation as \f$u \to -\infty\f$. With auxiliary quantities \f$r = (1 + u^2)^{-1/2} = 1/\operatorname{hypot}(1, u)\f$ and \f$s = u r\f$ (\f$|s| < 1\f$, \f$r^2 = 1 - s^2\f$), derivatives are \f$h^{(k)}(u) = \frac{1}{\pi} r^{k+1} W_{k-1}(s)\f$, evaluated in \f$s^2\f$ for \f$s^2 < 1/2\f$ and in \f$r^2\f$ for \f$s^2 \ge 1/2\f$:
  - \f$W_0(s) = 1\f$
  - \f$W_1(s) = -2s\f$
  - \f$W_2(s) = 8s^2 - 2 = 6 - 8r^2\f$
  - \f$W_3(s) = s(24 - 48s^2) = s(48r^2 - 24)\f$
  - \f$W_4(s) = 24 - 288s^2 + 384s^4 = 120 - 480r^2 + 384r^4\f$
  - \f$W_5(s) = s(-720 + 3840s^2 - 3840s^4) = s(-720 + 3840r^2 - 3840r^4)\f$
- Inverse log-Jacobian: \f$\log|h'(u)| = -\log\pi - \log(1 + u^2)\f$. Derivatives are \f$-2 r^{k} X_{k-1}(s)\f$, where \f$X_m(s)\f$ are evaluated in \f$s^2\f$ for \f$s^2 < 1/2\f$ and in \f$r^2\f$ for \f$s^2 \ge 1/2\f$:
  - \f$X_0(s) = s\f$
  - \f$X_1(s) = 1 - 2s^2 = 2r^2 - 1\f$
  - \f$X_2(s) = s(8s^2 - 6) = s(2 - 8r^2)\f$
  - \f$X_3(s) = -6 + 48s^2 - 48s^4 = -6 + 48r^2 - 48r^4\f$
  - \f$X_4(s) = s(120 - 480s^2 + 384s^4) = s(24 - 288r^2 + 384r^4)\f$
  - \f$X_5(s) = 120 - 2160s^2 + 5760s^4 - 3840s^6 = -120 + 2160r^2 - 5760r^4 + 3840r^6\f$

### Inverse and inverse square

For `inverse`: \f$g(v) = 1/v\f$, \f$h(u) = 1/u\f$.
- Forward and inverse: \f$g^{(k)}(v) = (-1)^k k!\,v^{-(k+1)}\f$.
- Forward and inverse log-Jacobians: \f$-2\log v\f$, with derivatives \f$2(-1)^k(k-1)!\,v^{-k}\f$ for \f$k \ge 1\f$.

For `inverse_sq`: \f$g(v) = 1/v^2\f$, \f$h(u) = 1/\sqrt{u}\f$.
- Forward: \f$g(v) = v^{-2}\f$, with derivatives \f$g^{(k)}(v) = (-1)^k(k+1)!\,v^{-(k+2)}\f$.
- Forward log-Jacobian: \f$\log 2 - 3\log v\f$, with derivatives \f$3(-1)^k(k-1)!\,v^{-k}\f$ for \f$k \ge 1\f$.
- Inverse: with \f$r = 1/\sqrt{u}\f$, \f$h^{(k)}(u) = c_k r^{2k+1}\f$ where \f$c_k = \prod_{j=0}^{k-1}(-\frac{1}{2} - j)\f$:
  - \f$c_0 = 1\f$, \f$c_1 = -1/2\f$, \f$c_2 = 3/4\f$, \f$c_3 = -15/8\f$, \f$c_4 = 105/16\f$, \f$c_5 = -945/32\f$, \f$c_6 = 10395/64\f$.
- Inverse log-Jacobian: \f$-\log 2 - \frac{3}{2}\log u\f$, with derivatives \f$\frac{3}{2}(-1)^k(k-1)!\,u^{-k}\f$ for \f$k \ge 1\f$.

### Square root

- Forward: \f$g(v) = \sqrt{v}\f$. Derivatives are computed as powers of \f$1/\sqrt{v}\f$: \f$g^{(k)}(v) = d_k (1/\sqrt{v})^{2k-1}\f$ where \f$d_k = \prod_{j=0}^{k-1}(\frac{1}{2} - j)\f$:
  - \f$d_0 = 1\f$, \f$d_1 = 1/2\f$, \f$d_2 = -1/4\f$, \f$d_3 = 3/8\f$, \f$d_4 = -15/16\f$, \f$d_5 = 105/32\f$, \f$d_6 = -945/64\f$.
- Forward log-Jacobian: \f$-\log 2 - \frac{1}{2}\log v\f$, with derivatives \f$\frac{1}{2}(-1)^k(k-1)!\,v^{-k}\f$ for \f$k \ge 1\f$.
- Inverse: \f$h(u) = u^2\f$, \f$h'(u) = 2u\f$, \f$h''(u) = 2\f$, and \f$h^{(k)}(u) = 0\f$ for \f$k \ge 3\f$.
- Inverse log-Jacobian: \f$\log 2 + \log u\f$, with derivatives \f$(-1)^{k-1}(k-1)!\,u^{-k}\f$ for \f$k \ge 1\f$.

### Inverse hyperbolic tangent

- Forward: \f$g(v) = \operatorname{atanh}(v) = \frac{1}{2}(\mathrm{log1p}(v) - \mathrm{log1p}(-v))\f$.
- Forward derivatives: with \f$a = 1/(1 - v)\f$ and \f$b = 1/(1 + v)\f$,
  \f[
  g^{(k)}(v) = \frac{(k-1)!}{2}\left(a^k + (-1)^{k-1} b^k\right)
  \f]
  For odd \f$k\f$: \f$\frac{(k-1)!}{2}(a^k + b^k)\f$.
  For even \f$k\f$: \f$(k-1)! v a b S_k(a, b)\f$.
- Forward log-Jacobian: \f$-\log(1 - v^2)\f$, computed as \f$-\mathrm{log1p}(-v^2)\f$ for \f$|v| < 1/2\f$ and \f$-\mathrm{log1p}(-v) - \mathrm{log1p}(v)\f$ for \f$|v| \ge 1/2\f$. For even \f$k \ge 2\f$: \f$(k-1)!(a^k + b^k)\f$; for odd \f$k \ge 1\f$: \f$2(k-1)! v a b S_k(a, b)\f$.
- Inverse: \f$h(u) = \tanh u\f$. With \f$e = \exp(-2|u|)\f$, \f$d = 1 + e\f$, \f$T = \mathrm{copysign}(-\mathrm{expm1}(-2|u|) / d, u)\f$, and \f$S = 1 - T^2 = 4e / d^2\f$, derivatives are \f$h^{(k)}(u) = S P_k(T)\f$, evaluated in \f$T^2\f$ for \f$T^2 < 1/2\f$ and in \f$S\f$ for \f$T^2 \ge 1/2\f$:
  - \f$P_1 = 1\f$
  - \f$P_2 = -2T\f$
  - \f$P_3 = 6T^2 - 2 = 4 - 6S\f$
  - \f$P_4 = T(16 - 24T^2) = T(24S - 8)\f$
  - \f$P_5 = 16 - 120T^2 + 120T^4 = 16 - 120S + 120S^2\f$
  - \f$P_6 = T(-272 + 960T^2 - 720T^4) = T(-32 + 480S - 720S^2)\f$
- Inverse log-Jacobian: \f$\log(1 - \tanh^2 u) = -2\log\cosh u\f$, with first derivative \f$-2\tanh u\f$ and higher derivatives \f$-2 h^{(k-1)}(u) = -2 S P_{k-1}(T)\f$ for \f$k \ge 2\f$.

### Inverse hyperbolic sine

- Forward: \f$g(v) = \mathrm{asinh}(v)\f$. With \f$r = (1 + v^2)^{-1/2}\f$ and \f$s = v r\f$, derivatives are \f$g^{(k)}(v) = r^k V_{k-1}(s)\f$, evaluated in \f$s^2\f$ for \f$s^2 < 1/2\f$ and in \f$r^2\f$ for \f$s^2 \ge 1/2\f$:
  - \f$V_0 = 1\f$
  - \f$V_1 = -s\f$
  - \f$V_2 = 3s^2 - 1 = 2 - 3r^2\f$
  - \f$V_3 = s(9 - 15s^2) = s(15r^2 - 6)\f$
  - \f$V_4 = 9 - 90s^2 + 105s^4 = 24 - 120r^2 + 105r^4\f$
  - \f$V_5 = s(-225 + 1050s^2 - 945s^4) = s(-120 + 840r^2 - 945r^4)\f$
- Forward log-Jacobian: \f$\log r = -\frac{1}{2}\log(1 + v^2)\f$, with derivatives \f$-r^k X_{k-1}(s)\f$ for \f$k \ge 1\f$, with \f$X_m\f$ as defined for cauchit.
- Inverse: \f$h(u) = \sinh u\f$, with derivatives \f$h^{(k)}(u) = \sinh u\f$ for even \f$k\f$, and \f$\cosh u\f$ for odd \f$k\f$.
- Inverse log-Jacobian: \f$\log\cosh u\f$, with first derivative \f$\tanh u\f$ and higher derivatives \f$S P_{k-1}(T)\f$ for \f$k \ge 2\f$.

### Complementary log-log

- Forward: with \f$t = \mathrm{log1p}(-v) = \log(1 - v)\f$, \f$g(v) = \log(-t)\f$.
- Forward derivatives: with \f$c = 1 / ((1 - v)t)\f$, \f$g^{(k)}(v) = (-1)^k C_k(t) c^k\f$:
  - \f$C_1(t) = 1\f$
  - \f$C_2(t) = -(1 + t)\f$
  - \f$C_3(t) = 2 + 3t + 2t^2\f$
  - \f$C_4(t) = -(6 + 12t + 11t^2 + 6t^3)\f$
  - \f$C_5(t) = 24 + 60t + 70t^2 + 50t^3 + 24t^4\f$
  - \f$C_6(t) = -(120 + 360t + 510t^2 + 450t^3 + 274t^4 + 120t^5)\f$
- Forward log-Jacobian: \f$-t - \log(-t)\f$, with derivatives \f$(-1)^k E_k(t) c^k\f$, where \f$E_k(t) = -C_k(t) + (-1)^k(k-1)!\,t^k\f$:
  - \f$E_1(t) = -(1 + t)\f$
  - \f$E_2(t) = 1 + t + t^2\f$
  - \f$E_3(t) = -(2 + 3t + 2t^2 + 2t^3)\f$
  - \f$E_4(t) = 6 + 12t + 11t^2 + 6t^3 + 6t^4\f$
  - \f$E_5(t) = -(24 + 60t + 70t^2 + 50t^3 + 24t^4 + 24t^5)\f$
  - \f$E_6(t) = 120 + 360t + 510t^2 + 450t^3 + 274t^4 + 120t^5 + 120t^6\f$
- Inverse: with \f$w = e^u\f$, \f$h(u) = 1 - e^{-w} = -\mathrm{expm1}(-w)\f$. Derivatives are \f$h^{(k)}(u) = \exp(u - w) P_k(w)\f$ for \f$w \le 1000\f$ (returning \f$0.0\f$ for \f$w > 1000\f$ where \f$\exp(u - w)\f$ underflows):
  - \f$P_1(w) = 1\f$
  - \f$P_2(w) = 1 - w = -\mathrm{expm1}(u)\f$ (evaluated via \f$\mathrm{expm1}\f$ to avoid cancellation near \f$u = 0\f$)
  - \f$P_3(w) = 1 - 3w + w^2\f$
  - \f$P_4(w) = 1 - 7w + 6w^2 - w^3\f$
  - \f$P_5(w) = 1 - 15w + 25w^2 - 10w^3 + w^4\f$
  - \f$P_6(w) = 1 - 31w + 90w^2 - 65w^3 + 15w^4 - w^5\f$
- Inverse log-Jacobian: \f$u - e^u\f$. First derivative is \f$-\mathrm{expm1}(u)\f$; higher derivatives are \f$-e^u\f$ for \f$k \ge 2\f$.

### Log-log

- Forward: with \f$t = \log v\f$ and \f$c = 1 / (v t)\f$, \f$g(v) = -\log(-t)\f$, with derivatives \f$g^{(k)}(v) = -C_k(t) c^k\f$ using the polynomials \f$C_k(t)\f$ from complementary log-log.
- Forward log-Jacobian: \f$-t - \log(-t)\f$, with derivatives \f$E_k(t) c^k\f$ using \f$E_k(t)\f$ from complementary log-log.
- Inverse: \f$h(u) = \exp(-e^{-u}) = 1 - h_{\mathrm{cloglog}}(-u)\f$, with derivatives \f$h^{(k)}(u) = (-1)^{k+1} h_{\mathrm{cloglog}}^{(k)}(-u)\f$.
- Inverse log-Jacobian: \f$-u - e^{-u}\f$. First derivative is \f$\mathrm{expm1}(-u)\f$; higher derivatives are \f$(-1)^{k+1} e^{-u}\f$ for \f$k \ge 2\f$.

### Affine

Let \f$\mu = p_1\f$ (location) and \f$\sigma = p_2 \ne 0\f$ (scale), with \f$q = 1/\sigma\f$.
- Forward: \f$g(v) = (v - \mu)/\sigma = (v - \mu) q\f$.
  - First derivative: \f$g'(v) = q\f$, and higher derivatives \f$g^{(k)}(v) = 0\f$ for \f$k \ge 2\f$.
  - Forward log-Jacobian: \f$\log|g'(v)| = -\log|\sigma|\f$. Derivations in argument are \f$0\f$; derivatives in \f$\sigma\f$ are \f$\frac{\partial^k}{\partial\sigma^k}\log|\sigma|^{-1} = (-1)^k(k-1)!/\sigma^k\f$.
- Inverse: \f$h(u) = \mu + \sigma u\f$.
  - First derivative: \f$h'(u) = \sigma\f$, and higher derivatives \f$h^{(k)}(u) = 0\f$ for \f$k \ge 2\f$.
  - Inverse log-Jacobian: \f$\log|h'(u)| = \log|\sigma|\f$.
- Numerical choices: the difference \f$v - \mu\f$ is formed as a single subtraction, which is exact when \f$v\f$ and \f$\mu\f$ are close. Mixed partials are linear in \f$v, u, \mu\f$ and powers of \f$q\f$, avoiding cancellation.

### Power

Let \f$\lambda = p_1 \ne 0\f$ and \f$\mu = 1/\lambda\f$.
- Forward: \f$g(v) = v^\lambda\f$.
  - Forward derivatives: \f$g^{(k)}(v) = F_k v^{\lambda - k}\f$ where \f$F_k = \prod_{j=0}^{k-1}(\lambda - j)\f$.
  - Forward log-Jacobian: \f$\log|g'(v)| = \log|\lambda| + (\lambda - 1)\log v\f$, with derivatives \f$(\lambda - 1)(-1)^{k-1}(k-1)! v^{-k}\f$ for \f$k \ge 1\f$.
- Inverse: \f$h(u) = u^\mu\f$.
  - Inverse derivatives: \f$h^{(k)}(u) = G_k u^{\mu - k}\f$ where \f$G_k = \prod_{j=0}^{k-1}(\mu - j) = \prod_{j=0}^{k-1}(1 - j\lambda)/\lambda\f$.
  - Inverse log-Jacobian: \f$\log|h'(u)| = -\log|\lambda| + (\mu - 1)\log u\f$, with \f$\mu - 1 = (1 - \lambda)/\lambda\f$.
- Numerical choices:
  - The terms \f$v^{\lambda - k}\f$ and \f$u^{\mu - k}\f$ are evaluated by splitting the exponent as \f$s + e\f$ without rounding loss using Knuth's two-sum algorithm, computing \f$x^s(1 + e\log x)\f$.
  - When \f$x^s\f$ underflows or overflows while \f$F_k x^s\f$ remains finite, the exponent is halved and factors are multiplied sequentially.
  - Falling factorial factors \f$\mu - j\f$ are computed via \f$\mathrm{fma}(-j, \lambda, 1)/\lambda\f$ to preserve relative accuracy when \f$\lambda \approx 1/j\f$.

### Softplus

Let \f$a = p_1 > 0\f$ (sharpness), \f$x = a v > 0\f$, \f$t = 1/\mathrm{expm1}(x)\f$, and \f$M = \log(1 - e^{-x})\f$.
- Forward: \f$g(v) = v + \frac{1}{a} M\f$.
  - First derivative: \f$g'(v) = 1 + t\f$.
  - Higher derivatives: \f$g^{(k)}(v) = a^{k-1} Q_{k-1}(t)\f$ for \f$k \ge 2\f$, where \f$Q_k(t)\f$ are derivative polynomials of \f$t\f$ with positive coefficients.
  - Forward log-Jacobian: \f$\log|g'(v)| = -M\f$, with derivatives \f$-a^k t^{(k-1)}(x)\f$.
- Inverse: let \f$x = a u\f$, \f$\sigma(x) = 1/(1 + e^{-x})\f$, and \f$S = \log(1 + e^x) = x + \log(1 + e^{-x})\f$ for \f$x > 0\f$.
  - Value: \f$h(u) = S/a\f$.
  - Derivatives: \f$h^{(k)}(u) = a^{k-1}\sigma^{(k-1)}(x)\f$ using the derivative polynomials of the logistic function.
  - Inverse log-Jacobian: \f$\log|h'(u)| = \log\sigma(x)\f$, with derivatives \f$-a^k\sigma^{(k-1)}(x)\f$.
- Numerical choices:
  - Pure \f$a\f$-derivatives cancel the \f$x\f$-linear parts of \f$Z = \log(e^x - 1)\f$ and \f$S\f$ analytically to avoid catastrophic cancellation.
  - Mixed partials of the forward log-Jacobian for \f$0 < x < 3\f$ are computed from the Bernoulli series of \f$\lambda(x) = -\log\operatorname{exprel}(-x)\f$ because its derivatives are \f$O(x)\f$ and the closed form cancels. For \f$x \ge 3\f$, the direct derivatives of \f$-\log(1 - e^{-x})\f$ are evaluated.

### Bounded

Let \f$a = p_1\f$ (lower bound) and \f$b = p_2\f$ (upper bound) with \f$a < b\f$.
Let \f$s = b - a\f$, \f$p = v - a\f$, \f$q = b - v\f$, \f$A = 1/p\f$, \f$B = 1/q\f$, \f$S = 1/s\f$, and \f$D = p - q = 2v - a - b\f$.
- Forward: \f$g(v) = \log(p/q)\f$.
  - Forward derivatives: for odd \f$k\f$, \f$g^{(k)}(v) = (k-1)!(A^k + B^k)\f$; for even \f$k\f$, \f$g^{(k)}(v) = (k-1)! D A B S_k(A, B)\f$ with \f$S_k(A, B) = \sum_{j=0}^{k-1} A^{k-1-j} B^j\f$.
  - Forward log-Jacobian: \f$\log|g'(v)| = \log((s/p)/q)\f$. For even \f$k \ge 2\f$, derivatives are \f$(k-1)!(A^k + B^k)\f$; for odd \f$k \ge 1\f$, \f$(k-1)! D A B S_k(A, B)\f$.
- Inverse: let \f$p_u = \operatorname{expit}(u)\f$, \f$q_u = \operatorname{expit}(-u)\f$, \f$U = p_u q_u\f$, and \f$D_u = q_u - p_u\f$.
  - Value: \f$h(u) = a q_u + b p_u\f$.
  - Derivatives: \f$h^{(k)}(u) = s \sigma_k(U, D_u)\f$ where \f$\sigma_k\f$ are the derivative polynomials of the logistic function.
  - Inverse log-Jacobian: \f$\log|h'(u)| = \log s + \log(p_u q_u)\f$. First derivative is \f$D_u\f$; higher derivatives are \f$-2\sigma_{k-1}(U, D_u)\f$ for \f$k \ge 2\f$.
- Distances to bounds: \f$b - h(u) = s q_u\f$ and \f$h(u) - a = s p_u\f$.
- Numerical choices:
  - Exact factoring \f$B - S = p S B\f$ and \f$A - S = q S A\f$ in mixed partials avoids differences of large opposing terms near domain boundaries, reducing maximum conditioned error from \f$10^{15}\f$ ulpC to 0.6 ulpC.
  - Evaluating \f$g(v)\f$ as \f$\log(p/q)\f$ rather than \f$\log p - \log q\f$ avoids 18 eps cancellation near the midpoint.

### Lower-bounded and upper-bounded

For `lower_bounded`: parameter \f$a = p_1\f$ (lower bound), with \f$t = v - a > 0\f$.
- Forward: \f$g(v) = \log t\f$, with derivatives \f$g^{(k)}(v) = (-1)^{k-1}(k-1)! t^{-k}\f$ for \f$k \ge 1\f$.
- Forward log-Jacobian: \f$\log|g'(v)| = -\log t = -g(v)\f$, with derivatives \f$-g^{(k)}(v)\f$.
- Inverse: \f$h(u) = a + e^u\f$, with derivatives \f$h^{(k)}(u) = e^u\f$ for all \f$k \ge 1\f$.
- Inverse log-Jacobian: \f$\log|h'(u)| = u\f$, with first derivative \f$1\f$ and higher derivatives \f$0\f$.
- Distance to lower bound: \f$h(u) - a = e^u\f$.
- Parameter partials: \f$g\f$ depends on \f$v - a\f$ only, so \f$\partial/\partial a = -\partial/\partial v\f$.

For `upper_bounded`: parameter \f$b = p_1\f$ (upper bound), with \f$s = b - v > 0\f$.
- Forward: \f$g(v) = -\log s\f$, with derivatives \f$g^{(k)}(v) = (k-1)! s^{-k}\f$ for \f$k \ge 1\f$.
- Forward log-Jacobian: \f$\log|g'(v)| = -\log s = g(v)\f$, with derivatives \f$g^{(k)}(v)\f$.
- Inverse: \f$h(u) = b - e^{-u}\f$, with derivatives \f$h^{(k)}(u) = (-1)^{k+1} e^{-u}\f$ for all \f$k \ge 1\f$.
- Inverse log-Jacobian: \f$\log|h'(u)| = -u\f$, with first derivative \f$-1\f$ and higher derivatives \f$0\f$.
- Distance to upper bound: \f$b - h(u) = e^{-u}\f$.
- Parameter partials: \f$g\f$ depends on \f$b - v\f$ only, so \f$\partial/\partial b = -\partial/\partial v\f$.

### Box-Cox

Let \f$\lambda = p_1\f$, \f$L = \log v\f$, \f$x = \lambda u\f$, \f$w = 1 + x\f$, \f$t = 1/w\f$, and \f$S = u\operatorname{log1prel}(\lambda u)\f$.
- Forward: \f$g(v) = L\operatorname{exprel}(\lambda L)\f$ for \f$|\lambda L| < 1\f$, and \f$(v^\lambda - 1)/\lambda\f$ beyond.
  - Argument derivatives: \f$g^{(k)}(v) = \prod_{j=1}^{k-1}(\lambda - j) v^{\lambda - k}\f$ for \f$k \ge 1\f$.
  - Parameter derivatives: \f$\frac{\partial^n}{\partial\lambda^n} g(v) = L^{n+1}\operatorname{exprel}^{(n)}(\lambda L)\f$.
  - Forward log-Jacobian: \f$\log|g'(v)| = (\lambda - 1) L\f$, with derivatives \f$(\lambda - 1)\frac{\mathrm{d}^k}{\mathrm{d}v^k}\log v\f$.
- Inverse: \f$h(u) = \exp(S)\f$.
  - Argument derivatives: \f$h^{(k)}(u) = P_k(\lambda)\exp((1 - k\lambda)S)\f$ with \f$P_k(\lambda) = \prod_{r=1}^{k-1}(1 - r\lambda)\f$.
  - Parameter derivatives: computed from \f$x_j = \frac{\partial^j}{\partial\lambda^j} S = u^{j+1}\operatorname{log1prel}^{(j)}(\lambda u)\f$ via partial Bell polynomials \f$A_n\f$ satisfying \f$\frac{\partial^n}{\partial\lambda^n} h = h A_n\f$.
  - Inverse log-Jacobian: \f$\log|h'(u)| = (1 - \lambda) S\f$.
- Distance to lower bound: \f$h(u)\f$ itself; distance to upper bound is \f$+\infty\f$.
- Numerical choices:
  - Formulations in \f$\operatorname{exprel}\f$ and \f$\operatorname{log1prel}\f$ prevent division by \f$\lambda\f$, yielding smooth evaluations across \f$\lambda = 0\f$ without branching.
  - Partial Bell polynomials and derivatives of \f$S\f$ are formed in a single pass across all components.

### Yeo-Johnson

Let \f$\lambda = p_1\f$, \f$c = 2 - \lambda\f$, \f$M = \mathrm{log1p}(v)\f$, \f$N = \mathrm{log1p}(-v)\f$, and \f$a = -u\f$.
- Forward:
  \f[
  g(v) = \begin{cases}
  M\operatorname{exprel}(\lambda M) = \frac{(1 + v)^\lambda - 1}{\lambda} & \text{for } v \ge 0 \\
  -N\operatorname{exprel}(c N) = -\frac{(1 - v)^c - 1}{c} & \text{for } v < 0
  \end{cases}
  \f]
  - Derivatives for \f$v \ge 0\f$: \f$g^{(k)}(v) = \prod_{j=1}^{k-1}(\lambda - j) (1 + v)^{\lambda - k}\f$.
  - Derivatives for \f$v < 0\f$: \f$g^{(k)}(v) = (-1)^{k-1}\prod_{j=0}^{k-2}(1 - \lambda - j) (1 - v)^{c - k}\f$.
  - Forward log-Jacobian: \f$(\lambda - 1) M\f$ for \f$v \ge 0\f$, and \f$(1 - \lambda) N\f$ for \f$v < 0\f$.
- Inverse:
  \f[
  h(u) = \begin{cases}
  \mathrm{expm1}(u\operatorname{log1prel}(\lambda u)) = (1 + \lambda u)^{1/\lambda} - 1 & \text{for } u \ge 0 \\
  -\mathrm{expm1}(a\operatorname{log1prel}(c a)) = 1 - (1 - c u)^{1/c} & \text{for } u < 0
  \end{cases}
  \f]
  - Derivatives for \f$u \ge 0\f$: \f$h^{(k)}(u) = \prod_{j=1}^{k-1}(1 - j\lambda)\exp(u(1 - k\lambda)\operatorname{log1prel}(\lambda u))\f$.
  - Derivatives for \f$u < 0\f$: \f$h^{(k)}(u) = \prod_{j=0}^{k-2}(1 - \lambda + j c)\exp(u(1 - \lambda + (k-1) c)\operatorname{log1prel}(c a))\f$.
  - Inverse log-Jacobian: \f$(1 - \lambda) u\operatorname{log1prel}(\lambda u)\f$ for \f$u \ge 0\f$, and \f$(1 - \lambda) u\operatorname{log1prel}(c a)\f$ for \f$u < 0\f$.
- Numerical choices:
  - Formulations in \f$\mathrm{log1p}\f$ and \f$\mathrm{expm1}\f$ with \f$\operatorname{exprel}\f$ and \f$\operatorname{log1prel}\f$ avoid cancellation near \f$v = 0\f$ and \f$u = 0\f$, and evaluate smoothly across \f$\lambda = 0\f$ and \f$\lambda = 2\f$ without limits or branching.

## Numerical considerations and implementation choices

The following numerical choices in `include/laplace/bijector/scalar.hpp` follow from measurements recorded in `docs/numerics.md`:

1. **Probit reciprocal density:** Computing \f$1/\phi(z)\f$ from \f$v\f$ via the Mills ratio \f$m(z) = \Phi(z)/\phi(z) = \sqrt{\pi/2}\operatorname{erfcx}(-z/\sqrt{2})\f$ for \f$v \le 1/2\f$ and \f$\sqrt{\pi/2}\operatorname{erfcx}(z/\sqrt{2})/(1 - v)\f$ for \f$v > 1/2\f$, rather than evaluating \f$\exp(z^2/2)\f$ at a rounded \f$z\f$. The rounding of \f$z\f$ would induce a relative error of \f$z^2 \varepsilon\f$ (reaching 1050 ulp at \f$v = 3.4 \times 10^{-118}\f$ for the second derivative, against 6.2 ulp with the Mills ratio).
2. **Expansion switching for hyperbolic and rational families:** The polynomials of \f$\tanh\f$ and of the \f$r, s\f$ family are evaluated in \f$T^2\f$ (or \f$s^2\f$) for values below \f$1/2\f$, and in \f$S = 1 - T^2\f$ (or \f$r^2 = 1 - s^2\f$) above \f$1/2\f$, where the \f$T^2\f$ forms suffer from cancellation with individual terms growing to 50 times the result (reducing maximum error from 48 ulpC to 8.6 ulpC).
3. **Log-cosh and log1p formulations:** \f$\log\cosh u\f$ below \f$|u| = 1\f$ is evaluated as \f$\mathrm{log1p}(e_m^2 / (2(1 + e_m)))\f$ where \f$e_m = \mathrm{expm1}(|u|)\f$, because \f$|u| + \mathrm{log1p}(e^{-2|u|}) - \log 2\f$ cancels to \f$u^2/2\f$ near zero (causing errors of \f$10^{17}\f$ ulp at \f$|u| = 10^{-9}\f$). Similarly, \f$-\log(1 - v^2)\f$ of atanh below \f$|v| = 1/2\f$ is computed as \f$-\mathrm{log1p}(-v^2)\f$.
4. **Power ordering:** Multiplications in \f$c a^k\f$ apply the coefficient \f$c\f$ first so that partial products do not become subnormal or overflow before the final result does. Derivatives of square root and of the inverse of inverse square are evaluated as integer powers of \f$1/\sqrt{\cdot}\f$ (preventing spurious NaN when \f$v < 10^{-102}\f$).
5. **Exact cancellation in derivative factors:** The factor \f$1 - w = 1 - e^u\f$ in the second derivative of the inverse of cloglog and loglog is computed as \f$-\mathrm{expm1}(u)\f$ (preventing \f$1.4 \times 10^8\f$ ulpC error at \f$u = -1.2 \times 10^{-9}\f$).
6. **Bounded cancellation avoidance near bounds:** In the mixed partials of the bounded map, the terms \f$B^k - S^k\f$ and \f$A^k - S^k\f$ (where \f$A = 1/(v-a)\f$, \f$B = 1/(b-v)\f$, and \f$S = 1/(b-a)\f$) cancel near the endpoints. Factoring \f$B - S = (v - a) S B\f$ and \f$A - S = (b - v) S A\f$ exactly with geometric sums reduces maximum conditioned error from \f$10^{15}\f$ ulpC to 0.6 ulpC.
7. **Knuth two-sum exponent splitting in power:** Exponents \f$\lambda - k\f$ and \f$\mu - k\f$ are represented as exact sums of two doubles \f$s + e\f$ using two-sum, evaluating \f$x^s (1 + e \log x)\f$. When \f$x^s\f$ would underflow or overflow while the scaled product remains finite, the exponent is halved.
8. **Softplus Bernoulli series and Leibniz forms:** Mixed partials of the forward log-Jacobian of softplus are evaluated via a Bernoulli series of \f$-\log\operatorname{exprel}(-x)\f$ for \f$0 < x < 3\f$, where closed-form differences cancel to \f$O(x)\f$. In the pure parameter derivatives of the value functions, the \f$x\f$-linear terms are cancelled analytically.
9. **Singularity-free Box-Cox and Yeo-Johnson via exprel and log1prel:** Box-Cox and Yeo-Johnson are formulated in \f$\operatorname{exprel}\f$ and \f$\operatorname{log1prel}\f$ and their derivatives, avoiding division by \f$\lambda\f$ or \f$2 - \lambda\f$. All orders evaluate smoothly through \f$\lambda = 0\f$ and \f$\lambda = 2\f$ without branching. For the inverse map, the Bell polynomials of the parameter derivatives of \f$S = u\operatorname{log1prel}(\lambda u)\f$ are computed in a single pass across all components.

## Measured accuracy

See \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured" for how accuracy figures, error metrics, and test bounds in this manual are defined and obtained.

Reference values for maps without parameters were computed using SymPy and evaluated with mpmath at precision growing with the point, each row computed at two precisions 120 bits apart and kept only if the two agree to \f$2^{-100}\f$ relative, across 52150 reference rows covering points log-uniform towards zero and domain bounds (down to \f$2^{-1000}\f$ and \f$1 - 2^{-54}\f$), uniform in the interior, and \f$|u|\f$ up to \f$2^{10}\f$ (\f$2^{1000}\f$ for asinh and inverse cauchit; `tools/reference/bijector_scalar.py`, `tests/bijector/test_scalar.cpp`).

Errors are given in ulpC (\ref accuracy_conditioned "conditioned scale"), with the scale \f$s = |f_k(x)| + |x f_{k+1}(x)|\f$ for the function \f$f_k\f$ of order \f$k\f$ at the point \f$x\f$, \f$f_{k+1}\f$ being the function of the next order; for the distances to the bounds, \f$f_{k+1}\f$ is \f$h'\f$.

The measured maximum error in ulpC over orders 0 to 6 on gcc (OpenMP) and CUDA (`docs/numerics.md`):

| Map | Forward | Inverse | Forward log-Jacobian | Inverse log-Jacobian |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| log | 3.84 / 3.84 | 0.574 / 0.574 | 3.84 / 3.84 | 0 / 0 |
| logit | 3.17 / 3.17 | 10.5 / 10.1 | 5.17 / 5.17 | 10.5 / 10.1 |
| probit | 6.17 / 6.17 | 2.28 / 2.28 | 6.57 / 6.57 | 0.832 / 0.832 |
| cauchit | 6.19 / 6.19 | 8.63 / 8.63 | 5.23 / 5.23 | 7.78 / 7.78 |
| cloglog | 6.73 / 3.12 | 9.98 / 7.61 | 4.16 / 3.34 | 0.969 / 0.969 |
| loglog | 4.58 / 3.23 | 11 / 8.49 | 4.84 / 3.38 | 0.883 / 0.883 |
| inverse | 3.92 / 3.92 | 3.25 / 3.25 | 3.92 / 3.92 | 3.25 / 3.25 |
| inverse_sq | 3.09 / 3.09 | 7.29 / 7.29 | 3.86 / 3.86 | 2.79 / 2.79 |
| sqrt | 10.9 / 10.9 | 0.246 / 0.246 | 2.43 / 2.43 | 2.95 / 2.95 |
| atanh | 2.78 / 2.78 | 2.67 / 1.67 | 3.53 / 5.05 | 2.67 / 1.67 |
| asinh | 8.55 / 9.49 | 0.494 / 0.622 | 5.35 / 7.85 | 1.9 / 2.27 |

In plain ulps, values and first derivatives (orders 0–1) are within 4.1 ulp on gcc, except for cloglog (up to 147 ulp) and loglog (up to 1430 ulp) whose values and first derivatives cross zero (loglog at \f$v = 1/e\f$) or are ill-conditioned (\f$\exp(u - e^u)\f$ carrying the rounding of \f$e^u\f$), where their ulpC is at most 2.01. Every quantity is within 11 ulpC on both gcc and CUDA. The accuracy of the distances to the bounds is given in their own section above.

For maps with parameters, reference values were generated by differentiating the definitions symbolically in the argument and parameters with SymPy and evaluating with mpmath at raised precision (`tools/reference/bijector_param_common.py`, `tests/bijector/param_common.hpp`). Errors are measured in ulpC with the \ref accuracy_conditioned "conditioned scale" \f$s = |f| + |x\,\partial f/\partial x| + |p_1\,\partial f/\partial p_1| + |p_2\,\partial f/\partial p_2|\f$.

The measured maximum error in ulpC over orders 0 to 6 on gcc and CUDA (`docs/numerics.md`):

| Map | Forward | Inverse | Forward log-Jacobian | Inverse log-Jacobian | Distances |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| affine | 0.362 / 0.362 | 0.5 / 0.5 | 0.495 / 0.495 | 0.495 / 0.495 | - |
| power | 4.76 / 4.37 | 5.59 / 4.93 | 5.88 / 5.88 | 5.87 / 5.87 | - |
| softplus | 7.67 / 7.04 | 4.38 / 4.25 | 18.4 / 18.4 | 3.48 / 3.47 | - |
| bounded | 1.1 / 1.1 | 7.09 / 5.82 | 1.1 / 1.1 | 7.09 / 5.82 | 0.721 / 0.721, 0.584 / 0.584 |
| lower_bounded | 0.679 / 0.679 | 0.725 / 0.725 | 0.679 / 0.679 | 0 / 0 | 0.273 / 0.273 |
| upper_bounded | 0.679 / 0.679 | 0.467 / 0.467 | 0.679 / 0.679 | 0 / 0 | 0.193 / 0.193 |
| box_cox | 7.97 / 9.71 | 42.8 / 46.3 | 1.28 / 1.28 | 49.4 / 43 | - |
| yeo_johnson | 26 / 26 | 24.9 / 39.9 | 3.06 / 3.06 | 46.3 / 27.3 | - |

The largest errors occur in the inverse of Box-Cox and its log-Jacobian at orders 4–6 (up to 49.4 ulpC, where \f$|\lambda u|\f$ is of order 1–10 and the mixed sums cancel), Yeo-Johnson forward map at \f$|v|\f$ near \f$10^{18}\f$ (26 ulpC) and its inverse log-Jacobian at order 6 (46.3 ulpC on gcc at \f$u = -3, \lambda = 0.4\f$), and softplus forward log-Jacobian at order 6 near \f$a v = 3\f$ (18.4 ulpC). Every quantity of every map is within 50 ulpC on both builds, and within 8 ulpC for all maps except Box-Cox, Yeo-Johnson, and the order 6 forward log-Jacobian of softplus.

